Title: Randomized Trial of Lecanemab in Early Alzheimer Disease
Authors: Christopher H. van Dyck, Chad J. Swanson, Paul Aisen
Year: 2023
DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa2212948
Abstract
Lecanemab, an IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds with high affinity to amyloid-beta soluble protofibrils, was tested in a phase 3 trial involving persons with early Alzheimer's disease.
Methods
We conducted an 18-month, multicenter, double-blind trial involving persons 50 to 90 years of age with early Alzheimer's disease.
Participants were randomly assigned in a 1:1 ratio to receive intravenous lecanemab or placebo every 2 weeks.
The primary end point was the change from baseline at 18 months in the score on the Clinical Dementia Rating-Sum of Boxes (CDR-SB).
Results
A total of 1795 participants were enrolled. The mean score on the CDR-SB at baseline was approximately 3.2.
Lecanemab reduced brain amyloid levels and resulted in less clinical decline on the CDR-SB than placebo at 18 months.
Conclusions
In persons with early Alzheimer's disease, lecanemab reduced markers of amyloid and resulted in moderately less decline on measures of cognition.
